+# Daily Grind security audit and deployment
+
+## Audit scope and findings
+
+Reviewed all application code: `server.js`, `index.html`, and `package.json`.
+The app is a static coffee storefront on Node's HTTP server. It has no runtime
+packages, scripts, DOM sinks, cookies, session store, payment/OAuth integrations,
+iframes, webhooks, or privileged browser features. Account and order routes are
+placeholder views of the same storefront, not authenticated services.
+The sole external asset is the existing HTTPS Unsplash background image.
+
+Addressed findings:
+
+- Missing response protections: added enforced CSP and Trusted Types, framing
+  controls, MIME sniffing prevention, referrer and permissions policies, COOP
+  `same-origin-allow-popups`, and CORP `same-origin` on every application response.
+- Inline CSS required unsafe policy exceptions: moved styles to `/styles.css`,
+  including the heading's style attribute. CSP allows only this origin's styles
+  and Unsplash images; no scripts, inline styles, plugins, frames, fonts, or
+  network API connections are permitted. No script nonce/hash is needed because
+  there are no scripts. Future scripts require a deliberate CSP update.
+- No reporting or safe rollout: added `CSP_MODE=report-only`, an HTTPS
+  `Reporting-Endpoints` group, CSP `report-to`, and a `report-uri` fallback.
+- Unbounded/untrusted telemetry risk: collector accepts only CSP/Reporting API
+  media types, limits requests to 16 KiB and 20 reports per batch, and limits
+  collection to 60 requests/minute per server process. Logs contain only type,
+  allowlisted directive, and disposition enums. It discards all URLs, query
+  strings, samples, user agents, and identifiers, including malicious values.
+- Unrestricted resource requests: Fetch Metadata rejects cross-site and sibling
+  subdomain subresource requests, preserving top-level GET/HEAD document
+  navigation and clients without those headers. Cache responses vary on all
+  three relevant metadata headers and use `no-store`.
+- Logout was a GET link: now a POST form with origin validation; only a successful
+  POST sends `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"`. This clears browser
+  data on supporting secure contexts; it is not session revocation. There are no
+  server sessions today. Add server-side revocation and CSRF protection appropriate
+  to the authentication model when implementing real accounts.
+- Arbitrary routes/methods returned HTML and Host parsing could throw: fixed
+  asset/page routing, 404/405 responses, bounded request timeouts, and fixed-base
+  request-target parsing. User input never becomes a filesystem path.
+
+## Run and validate
+
+Use Node.js 24 LTS. There are no package dependencies to install.
+
+```sh
+npm start
+npm run build
+```
+
+The build runs syntax validation and automated HTTP security tests. CI runs the
+same gate on pushes and pull requests. Checks cover enforcement/staging headers,
+asset compatibility, routing, hostile request targets, origin checks, resource
+isolation, report sanitization, body limits (including chunked bodies), and rate
+limits. No manual browser verification was performed. HTTP tests cannot establish
+browser enforcement compatibility or provide real production violation telemetry.
+
+## Production rollout
+
+Terminate TLS at a reverse proxy, redirect HTTP to HTTPS there, and prevent public
+access to the Node origin port. Set the exact public origin, including any
+nonstandard port. Production startup fails without an HTTPS origin; this validates
+configuration, not the proxy's actual TLS behavior. Forwarded headers are never
+trusted to enable HSTS or construct reporting URLs.
+
+```sh
+NODE_ENV=production APP_ORIGIN=https://coffee.example CSP_MODE=report-only npm start
+```
+
+1. Deploy to staging and run the build gate. Confirm through automated edge HTTP
+   checks that the proxy preserves the application headers, serves correct MIME
+   types, redirects HTTP, and routes POST `/security-reports` to this server.
+2. For an existing production deployment, start with `CSP_MODE=report-only`.
+   Framing, base-URI, and object restrictions remain enforced alongside companion
+   headers. The full candidate policy reports violations without blocking.
+   Default `CSP_MODE=enforce` is appropriate for this audited script-free app.
+3. Collect reports through representative real traffic for days to weeks. Review
+   high-frequency directive/disposition patterns, correlate with recent changes,
+   and distinguish extension noise. The privacy-preserving collector deliberately
+   omits source details; reproduce suspected regressions in staging without
+   enabling raw request-body logs. Reports are untrusted signals, not proof of
+   attacks. Export stdout to a retained monitoring system and alert on sustained
+   increases; absence of reports does not prove compatibility.
+4. Set `CSP_MODE=enforce` and redeploy once the candidate is compatible. Keep
+   reporting enabled. Roll back to `report-only` to diagnose policy regressions.
+   Policy rollback does not undo already cached HSTS.
+5. HSTS starts at `max-age=300` on configured HTTPS deployments. After validating
+   certificate renewal, redirects, and all required hosts, increase
+   `HSTS_MAX_AGE` gradually, up to `31536000`. `HSTS_MAX_AGE=0` stops future HSTS
+   caching after a successful HTTPS response. Neither `includeSubDomains` nor
+   preload is enabled; each requires a separate domain-wide readiness review.
+
+Configure proxy body limits, timeouts, and rate limits as well: the in-process
+report limiter is global, resets on restart, and is independent for each worker.
+It bounds overhead but an attacker can exhaust that worker's report allowance.
+Do not log collector request bodies at the proxy, tracing, or observability layer:
+browsers may send sensitive URLs and script samples before the app discards them.
+Use a dedicated origin if clearing cookies/storage would affect sibling apps.
+
+COEP and document isolation are intentionally omitted: this app does not require
+shared memory, and enforcing them could block third-party assets. CORS grants and
+SRI for scripts are unnecessary with no APIs or external scripts. If integrations,
+inline code, sessions, embeds, or browser permissions are added, repeat the audit,
+add appropriate automated coverage, and stage revised policies before enforcement.
+
+References: [MDN CSP](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP),
+[Reporting-Endpoints](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Reporting-Endpoints),
+and [Clear-Site-Data](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Clear-Site-Data).
